      "side": 1,
      "message": "question: does it work as expected when __hw_clock_source_read() wraps around zero?\niiuc tick is at 2^18 kHz, and the tick register is 32 bits (?), so will wrap around in \u003c5 hours. right?\n\nif so, then there\u0027s the following minor disadvantage of the current algo:\n1) we sent a response to a spi packet, deasserted \u0027ready\u0027 100us later, set last_time_deassert_ to \u003ccur_time\u003e.\n2) ~5 hours have passed. so, the reading of __hw_clock_source_read() is ~ \u003ccur_time\u003e again. we get a new command, process it, and call ap_start_ack_completion() when __hw_clock_source_read() \u003d\u003d last_time_deassert_. diff_usec is 100us, so all we do is schedule_timer_(100us), 100us later the timer will fire and *assert* \u0027ready\u0027, then 100us later deassert it. so, we are basically asserting ready 100us later than we should.\ngranted, this is a rare occurrence (5hr*N between calls w/o going to deep sleep) and a relatively minor inconvenience (100us lost), so I feel we can ignore this. but I\u0027d like to make sure this analysis was done and see it captured in the comments.\n\nso, I guess, nit: write a comment explaining what happens when it wraps around 0.\nor even better: write a markdown file with design and put under source control in cr50 tree, in this file refer to it.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 88,
        "startChar": 13,
        "endLine": 88,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "8699e54a8ff025aed919ae230df33cc30e66ef38",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
